WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
SmallDocs is a tool which lets any coding
agent create a document and open it in the
browser.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:07.760
So, for example, here I've been working on
an application,

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.480
I'm thinking of installing OpenID Connect
for users,

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.420
and I've got my agent to write a document
for me.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.140
And it opens in the browser.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:24.140
Or I'm on a coding agent, for example, one
of the more UI ones,

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.040
and I'm working with an agent on something,

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:30.100
and I want to read about it in a cleaner
environment,

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.120
and the agent can open a document here.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.200
So, underneath these documents they are...

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:47.140
They're markdown files, so they can be
saved in your repository or wherever they
are,

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:50.200
but they're also saved in our cloud as
well, which I'll get on to in a moment.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.460
The thinking behind SmallDocs is that these
chat areas where we do all of our work

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.440
today, they become very busy, very filled,
and very cluttered.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.160
They're rushing along, and you come up with
another question you want to ask,

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.280
and you ask your agent, and the chat moves
on.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:09.660
But sometimes your understanding isn't
moving along at the same pace as the chat.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.560
So there's a separation for me of the work
which is happening in these chats,

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.700
and the understanding which I do in
SmallDocs.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.760
And SmallDocs are designed to be a calm,
clean, fast,

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.500
lightweight, and spacious place to read and
gather thoughts,

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.580
but as well as be extremely flexible
documents with very, very deep
functionality.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:31.680
So I'll just give you some examples of the
kind of functionality that's available in
SmallDocs.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:36.220
So, first of all, the documents, they are
originally rendered collapsed,

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:40.200
so you can get an idea of what do I need to
look at now, and you can expand them all,

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.900
all the areas there, or expand the areas
one by one,

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.340
or hit the H1 and expand everything.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:53.800
They allow the agent to render very nice
diagrams, for example a Mermaid diagram,

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.940
and you can get everything in its own nice
interface.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:00.420
And one of the things that is also really
important about SmallDocs

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.000
is agent-to-human feedback loops.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.460
So all the little features come with things
that make your life easier to either

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.360
communicate ideas to humans or communicate
ideas to other agents or to the agent that
you're working with.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.780
So, for example, on a diagram, you can copy
the source and then discuss it with an
agent.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:17.800
For example, I could paste that in there,
or I can copy the diagram and I can send
that into a chat.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:22.760
It can be a small little thing. So, for
example, when an agent's made a table and I
want to copy a column

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.200
or I want to copy the whole table as a CSV
or copy the whole table as a PNG, I can do
that too.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:32.140
So they're filled with little useful things
to make your life easier, basically.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:34.600
Here's another example of a SmallDoc.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.000
You can see another Mermaid diagram.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.360
They render charts. They render math. They
render multi-tab spreadsheets.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.700
So an agent can actually do a bit of work
for you

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:45.580
and you can treat that as an actual
spreadsheet and interact with it

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.620
and do what you need with it.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:53.920
The idea is that we keep tackling super
kind of, we tackle

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.180
so many different tasks with our agents.
And the document format that we work with,
I think,

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.220
should be as flexible as this vast range of
tasks

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:03.220
and not siloed into this is a spreadsheet,
this is some slides, this is some code.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.560
I think everything lives together.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:05.960
And in SmallDocs, that's how it works.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:09.580
And I found that that really allows me to
get the agent to communicate to me

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:13.360
in an effective way that I can understand
better. And of course, we render code.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.180
The agent can make extremely visually
appealing slides and it has really good
defaults built in.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:25.260
So if you just ask for a deck, it can
generate nice looking presentations, which
are,

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:29.120
I think, very human readable. And you can
even use presentations not to present to
someone.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.740
I use presentations a lot to understand
things.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:34.360
So I'll say to the agent, okay, make one
slide for me for this idea,

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.540
all the different parts of this idea, and I
can ingest it one by one.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:42.220
So it's kind of a creative tool to allow
your agent to communicate to you in a way
that

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:44.220
maximizes your rate of understanding
because we all have

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:47.900
so much to understand these days with
different work tasks that are very
complicated

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:53.820
with the agent who is extremely clever
doing work beyond our kind of where we're
at at that moment.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.520
So they're deep, they're flexible, they're
fast, and they're lightweight.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.920
SmallDocs also comes with a library.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:04.340
And every library has a personal, every
user has a personal library, which you
can't add anyone to.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:05.720
So this is just for your personal
documents.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:08.880
And there's also, you can create as many as
you want, team libraries.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:15.360
And you can search for documents in the
library, for example, penguins.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.200
And you can do things like filter for
starred documents, this kind of stuff.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.740
So it's a nice place where as you're
working with an agent,

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:23.700
you're creating all these documents, you're
creating these kind of isolated blocks of
thinking

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:25.860
which you might share with other people or
just just for yourself.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.940
And you know that everything you've created
is saved and you can go back to it.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.680
You can, from this page, choose the library
that you want the document to be associated
with.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:37.060
You can choose who you want to share it
with.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:40.300
And you can update the tags of the document
to match what you want.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:44.580
But the agent is instructed to build a
tagged knowledge base for you.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:48.620
So you won't have to do anything. But just
by doing all your different bits of
thinking, creating documents,

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:53.660
you will start to build a knowledge base
that you can query and also the agent can
query.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:55.340
So the agent with your permissions,

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.320
as in it can't read things that other
people have created that haven't shared
with you,

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:04.420
can use the tool to go to your library and
find what's available to you so you can get
things going.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:06.800
And so that's where sharing documents
within your library.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.740
And you can also share them outside of your
library,

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:10.380
either a page that can be indexed by Google

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:13.200
or a private link that you can just share
with someone that Google can't pick up.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:19.400
It's also possible to work with an agent

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:24.420
to get feedback going on. So let's use this
document.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:31.320
And so I could say something like to the
agent.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:35.440
This area isn't clear.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:38.680
Instead, use two slides.

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:40.540
And you can use voice to speak if you want
to.

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:44.640
So now I've left a comment for the agent.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.660
And I can either if I want just copy with
comments and the agent

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:50.500
and paste it into the agent chat box, or if
you want, the agent can also pick up on
comments.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:54.440
So I could say clear this text box.

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:06.480
And the agent can interact with this cloud
document.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:08.140
And it can find the comment.

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:12.700
And then it can update it. And as the agent
updates it, you'll get a...

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:14.540
it'll change the document we're working on
together.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:16.960
And you'll get a change log.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:20.600
And the agent will also leave a reason as
to why it's changed things.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:24.740
While we're waiting for that, it's also
just worth pointing out little small things
like

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:28.620
the ability to copy things and that with
comments, you can leave them in a very
detailed way.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:32.520
So I could leave comment on a specific part
of this diagram, for example,

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:34.260
if I wanted more information on it.

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:39.000
So let's see, I'm sure the agent is working
away.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:42.620
And now it's editing the document with our
updated request.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:49.240
The document's updated. And now we can see
it's made those slides there.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:52.650
And we have a change log where the agent
has

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.420
updated things with two inline slides.

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:58.020
So you can check how things have changed.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:04.220
One other little thing is here you can see
shared tags from your library and recent.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:10.820
The project is also open source. So the
code is on GitHub. Thanks very much.
